1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,860
Look at Orion this winter at
the orange star on its shoulder.

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:05,700
That star is dying.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,480
So is Betelgeuse about to explode?

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:10,640
And what would you see if it did?

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:13,340
It is called Betelgeuse.

6
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:15,880
One day it will blow itself apart,

7
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:19,760
and for a few weeks it will
shine so brightly that you could

8
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,400
see it in the daytime sky.

9
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:25,260
In this film, I will show
you what kind of star it is,

10
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:28,280
why it scared astronomers in 2019,

11
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,200
how a giant star actually dies,

12
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,360
what the night it goes would
look like from your garden,

13
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:36,940
and the honest answer to when.

14
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:40,120
Betelgeuse is a red supergiant.

15
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:41,860
It is enormous.

16
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:45,400
Put it where the Sun is, and
its surface would reach out past

17
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:48,580
the orbit of Mars and out
beyond the asteroid belt.

18
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:52,680
It is somewhere between five and
seven hundred light-years away.

19
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:57,020
Even measuring that is hard
because the star is so big and so

20
00:00:57,140 --> 00:01:00,160
restless that its centre
is difficult to pin down,

21
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:01,740
and it is young.

22
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:06,180
Our Sun is four and a half billion
years old and halfway through

23
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:06,760
its life.

24
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:10,460
Betelgeuse is only about
ten million years old,

25
00:01:10,860 --> 00:01:12,840
and it is already near
the end of its life.

26
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:14,800
Why so fast?

27
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:16,540
Because it is heavy.

28
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:21,120
With somewhere around fifteen
to twenty times the Sun's mass,

29
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:23,600
it burns its fuel ferociously.

30
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:27,940
It gives out around a hundred
thousand times as much light as the

31
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,360
Sun.

32
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:31,920
Big stars live fast and die young.

33
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:34,060
You can find it yourself.

34
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:38,080
On a winter evening, look for
the three stars of Orion's Belt,

35
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:40,040
then go up and to the left.

36
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:42,720
Its surface is cooler than the Sun's,

37
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:45,480
and that is why it glows
orange instead of white.

38
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:49,200
At the end of 2019,
something strange happened.

39
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:52,940
Betelgeuse, normally one of
the brightest stars in the sky,

40
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:54,200
began to fade.

41
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,480
By February 2020, it had dropped
to about a third of its usual

42
00:01:58,500 --> 00:01:59,140
brightness.

43
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:03,160
It was the faintest anyone had
measured it in more than fifty years.

44
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:05,600
The question went all around the world.

45
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:06,840
Was this it?

46
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:09,380
Was Betelgeuse about to explode?

47
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:10,980
It was not.

48
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,040
Telescopes caught the answer.

49
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:16,980
A huge bubble of gas had burst
out from the star's surface and

50
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:17,800
drifted away.

51
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,740
As it cooled, it turned
into a cloud of dust,

52
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:23,560
and the dust blocked part
of the star from our view.

53
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:27,160
A cool patch on the surface
made the dimming deeper.

54
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:29,260
Why did it fool everyone?

55
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:32,680
Because from Earth, all we
see is a point of light.

56
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:37,080
When that point fades, the obvious guess
is that something terrible is happening

57
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:37,760
inside.

58
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:40,540
By the spring, Betelgeuse was back.

59
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:43,120
But the scare taught
astronomers something.

60
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:46,040
A dying giant does not fade quietly.

61
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,980
It throws off pieces of itself as it goes.

62
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,080
So how does a star like this die?

63
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,160
All its life, a star is a balance.

64
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,200
Gravity pulls everything in.

65
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:00,880
The heat of nuclear fusion in
the core pushes everything out.

66
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,520
For most of its life, Betelgeuse
fused hydrogen into helium.

67
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,680
Now it is fusing helium into carbon.

68
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:12,080
After that will come heavier
and heavier elements,

69
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:15,020
each stage burning faster than the last.

70
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:17,900
The final stages take only years,

71
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:19,080
then days.

72
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:21,420
The end of the line is iron.

73
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,220
Fusing iron does not release energy.

74
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:25,700
It costs energy.

75
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,000
So when the core fills with iron,

76
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:31,060
the fire goes out, and gravity wins.

77
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:36,120
In less than a second, a core
heavier than the Sun collapses into

78
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:37,800
a ball the size of a city.

79
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,180
The outer layers fall in, bounce off it,

80
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:43,320
and are blasted out into space.

81
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,580
That is a supernova.

82
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,740
What is left behind is a neutron star

83
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,020
so dense that a teaspoon of
it would weigh as much as a

84
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:52,620
mountain.

85
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:55,500
If you saw our film on neutron stars,

86
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,840
you know how strange that place is.

87
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,880
Or if the core is heavy
enough, a black hole.

88
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:04,260
We make one of these every week.

89
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:07,180
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

90
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:10,960
So imagine the night Betelgeuse explodes.

91
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,380
The first sign would not be light at all.

92
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:19,200
It would be a flood of ghostly
particles called neutrinos

93
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:21,700
streaming out of the collapsing core.

94
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,420
They pass straight through the Earth.

95
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:28,640
Detectors deep underground
would catch a sudden burst,

96
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:33,100
and astronomers would get the alert
hours before the star brightened.

97
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:35,720
Then the light arrives.

98
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:41,220
Within days, Betelgeuse would
blaze as bright as the Moon at

99
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:42,160
half phase.

100
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:44,640
You could see it in broad daylight.

101
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:45,820
At night,

102
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:50,040
it would outshine everything
in the sky except the Moon.

103
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:52,920
It would stay that bright for weeks,

104
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:56,500
then fade slowly over months and years

105
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,780
until the shoulder of
Orion was simply gone.

106
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:02,900
The hunter would lose a shoulder,

107
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:08,140
and every person who looked up
afterwards would see a different Orion

108
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:09,460
from the one we know.

109
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:11,720
Should anyone worry?

110
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:13,000
No.

111
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:17,840
A supernova only harms life
on Earth if it is within a few

112
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:18,900
dozen light-years.

113
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:21,960
Betelgeuse is far beyond that.

114
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:23,840
We would get the show,

115
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:25,460
not the danger.

116
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:28,200
And here is a strange thought.

117
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:32,580
The light from Betelgeuse
takes centuries to reach us.

118
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:36,740
If it had exploded sometime
in the late Middle Ages,

119
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:39,000
more than five hundred years ago,

120
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:42,140
we would only be finding out about now.

121
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:44,460
So when will it happen?

122
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:50,100
The best estimates say Betelgeuse has
around a hundred thousand years left.

123
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,760
That is the answer most
astronomers would give you.

124
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:57,620
Not in our lifetimes, almost certainly.

125
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,220
Why can't we just look and tell?

126
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:05,840
Because the changes that
matter happen deep in the core,

127
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:09,620
and the surface barely shows
them until the very end.

128
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:15,260
From outside, a star with 100,000
years left and a star with a

129
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:18,300
hundred years left can
look almost the same.

130
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:21,520
And the star keeps surprising us.

131
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:26,700
A few researchers have argued that
it could be much further along than

132
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:27,220
we think

133
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,580
and might go within centuries.

134
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:32,000
Most do not agree.

135
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:36,380
The honest answer is that
nobody can give you a date,

136
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:40,900
and in twenty twenty-five,
astronomers reported something new:

137
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:46,240
a faint glimmer right where a hidden
companion star was predicted to be.

138
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:50,900
A small star circling Betelgeuse
every six years or so,

139
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:52,740
lost in its glare.

140
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:54,820
If it is real,

141
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:59,620
it explains a slow pulse in
Betelgeuse's light that has puzzled

142
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:01,120
astronomers for decades.

143
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:04,300
The sighting is still tentative.

144
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:09,340
The best chance to check comes in
late twenty twenty-seven when the two

145
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:11,080
stars are furthest apart.

146
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,000
We have watched stars explode before.

147
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:17,580
In the year ten fifty-four,

148
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:22,720
astronomers in China recorded a
new star so bright that it could be

149
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:25,480
seen in daylight for
more than three weeks.

150
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:28,900
Today, at that spot in the sky,

151
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:30,760
there is the Crab Nebula,

152
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:32,300
still expanding.

153
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:36,300
So is Betelgeuse about to explode?

154
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:38,040
Not tonight,

155
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:40,920
and probably not for a very long time,

156
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:42,420
but it will.

157
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:44,060
And when it does,

158
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:45,820
it will give something back.

159
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:51,160
Much of the oxygen you breathe
and the calcium in your bones were

160
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:53,980
made inside giant stars like this one

161
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:57,040
and scattered across
space when they exploded.

162
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:59,680
Betelgeuse will do the same,

163
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:04,260
seeding the clouds that will
become new stars and new worlds.

164
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:09,020
What if the most important
thing about a dying star

165
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:10,740
is not how it ends,

166
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:12,760
but what it leaves behind?

167
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:16,360
The bigger question is closer to home.

168
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:18,020
Long ago,

169
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:21,240
giant stars near a cloud of gas and dust

170
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:23,600
seeded it with new elements.

171
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:27,700
That cloud became our Sun, our Earth,

172
00:08:27,980 --> 00:08:28,700
and us.

173
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:33,800
We are made of stars that lived
and died before the Sun was

174
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:34,260
born.
